// cylinder_sedimentation_showcase_pureanalytical_10k_floaterhistory_gas.prm
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// "PURE KODAM+GUO" SIBLING of cylinder_sedimentation_showcase_analytical_10k_floaterhistory_gas.prm:
// identical dense 100-log pile/scenario/settings, only usePureAnalyticalCylinder added (true), which
// removes the Cylinder-Cylinder GJK/EPA safety net entirely -- cylinderCylinderResultIsUncertain()'s
// verdict is ignored, and the closed-form Kodam et al. (2010) + Guo et al. (2012) dispatch is
// trusted as the sole answer, exactly as those two papers' own algorithm stands on its own (neither
// paper uses GJK/EPA or any other iterative solver anywhere -- confirmed by reading both papers
// directly, plus Guo et al.'s corrections paper). The raw-center-distance guard in
// SolidCylinderContactDetection (added after the false-positive band-band-skewed crash this exact
// scenario originally hit -- see the analytical sibling's own header/job history) stays active: it
// is not part of either paper's scenario dispatch, just a cheap pre-filter, so running "pure" mode
// with it on is still an honest reproduction of the papers' own methodology. See
// SimulationParams.h's ModelParams::usePureAnalyticalCylinder doc comment for the full rationale.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// FLOATER-HISTORY-CSV DIAGNOSTIC VARIANT of cylinder_sedimentation_showcase_analytical_gas.prm:
// identical dense 100-log pile/scenario, only two changes -- timesteps cut from 100000 to
// 10000 (this scenario's ContactHistoryNotification race has historically fired around step
// 929, so 10000 gives generous room to catch it, at a fraction of the wall-clock cost of the
// full run) and ModelParameters.logFloaterHistory turned on, so every rank writes its own
// floater-history/rank_<N>.csv (per-contact: computing rank vs. mesa_pd's own getOwner()
// "global" owner, detection path -- analytical scenario vs. GJK/EPA tier -- force delta) and
// floater-history/rank_<N>_state.csv (per-floater world-state snapshot, local AND ghost
// copies, every sub-cycle). Built specifically to catch this scenario's own race with the new
// logger rather than relying on the coarser [ContactHistory DUPLICATE]/[CylComputation]
// diagnostics alone. See SimulationParams.h's ModelParams::logFloaterHistory doc comment for
// the full column/rationale.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ANALYTICAL-CONTACT-DETECTION VARIANT of cylinder_sedimentation_showcase_gas.prm,
// identical in every other respect. Adds useAnalyticalContactDetection true to
// ModelParameters, routing every Cylinder x Cylinder contact this file's dense
// 100-log pile (5x4x5 grid, 12 LU spacing, "end-on and side-on collisions ...
// expected", per the header below) generates through PaperAnalyticalContacts.h's
// cylinderCylinderContact (Kodam et al. 2010 + Guo et al. 2012's corrections)
// instead of the default GJK/EPA fallback. Unlike Box-Box, this is a HYBRID path
// -- cylinderCylinderResultIsUncertain() gates every analytical result and falls
// back to GJK/EPA for the oblique-tilt cases (face-edge/special-contact/band-edge
// scenarios) the papers' own taxonomy can misjudge in magnitude -- so this run is
// a real stress test of BOTH the analytical dispatch and the fallback gate under
// a genuinely dense, tumbling pile, complementing (not substituting for)
// PaperAnalyticalContactTest.cpp's 33 unit-test cases. See
// ModelParameters.useAnalyticalContactDetection (SimulationParams.h) and
// ParticleKernels.h's SolidCylinderContactDetection Cylinder x Cylinder overload
// for the wiring.
//
// PARTICLE IMPACT AND SEDIMENTATION AT A FREE SURFACE -- CYLINDER ("log")
// variant of sphere_sedimentation_showcase_gas.prm (v2). Identical setup
// (grid, spacing, densities, domain, timesteps, no pillars, X/Y periodic) with
// the floater shape swapped from spheres to short logs, to compare how a
// curved-but-anisotropic shape sediments/separates vs. the sphere baseline.
//
// Shape: radius 4.5, length 9 (half-length 4.5), axis <0,1,0> (the project's
// standard spanwise-log orientation) -- chosen so the cylinder's diameter (9)
// and axial length (9) both match the sphere's diameter, keeping it as close
// to isotropic as a finite cylinder can be and preserving the same 12 LU grid
// spacing / clearance used for the sphere and box variants. Logs are free to
// translate AND rotate (no pinning, no initial tilt beyond the default axis
// orientation), so end-on and side-on collisions during impact/sedimentation
// are expected and are part of what this variant tests.
//
// Densities: 3-class mix (0.5 light / 1.0 neutral / 2.0 heavy), now assigned
// per X-COLUMN rather than mixed across the full (i,j,k) grid index: every
// log at a given X position shares the same density regardless of its Y/Z
// position, cycling through the 3 classes as X increases (densities[i % 3]
// over the 5 X-columns) -- x=40/76 -> 0.5, x=52/88 -> 1.0, x=64 -> 2.0.
//
// Grid: 5 (X) x 4 (Y) x 5 (Z) = 100 logs, spacing 12 LU. Bottom layer centred
// at z=64 (just above the waterline), top layer at z=112.
//
// Domain: <128, 60, 180> / cellsPerBlock <32, 20, 30> = 4×3×6 = 72 processes
//
// v3 -- staticReferenceHeightOverride experiment: the flat MEM hydrostatic reference
// height (absoluteLiquidHeight, the design-time estimate ~54-56 from liquidHeightFactor)
// never updates as the run progresses. Measured directly from the v2 (Bogner-fixed)
// run's own clean, high-coherence plane-fits in the last ~500 sec of the run
// (steps ~93,000-100,000, n=36 samples, range 64.28-65.50): the REAL water level had
// risen to ~65.08, from ~100 settled cylinders displacing that much volume. That ~9-10
// LU of uncancelled head is exactly the deltaH driving the phantom MEM lift that
// launched cylinders ballistically out of the pile (see changes.md). This run overrides
// the static reference to that measured end-state value (65.08) as a direct ablation
// test: if the ballistic launches and the too-high final equilibrium (light cylinders
// settling at ~2% submersion instead of ~50%) are driven by the stale flat reference,
// using the correct end-of-run height throughout should visibly reduce both.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

BlockForestParameters
{
   cellsPerBlock                 < 32, 20, 30 >;
   periodicity                   < 1, 1, 0 >;   // X and Y periodic — no side walls, only Z floor/ceiling
   loadSnapshot                  false;
   storeSnapshot                 true;
   snapshotFrequency             10000;
   snapshotBaseFolder            snapshots;
}

DomainParameters
{
   domainSize         <128, 60, 180>;
   wavePeriods        1;
   liquidHeightFactor 2.862;
   floorHeightFactor  4.1393;
   initialAmplitude   0;
}

PIDParameters
{
   targetMeanVelocityMagnitude 0;       // still water — no flow forcing
   proportionalGain            2e-4;
   derivativeGain              1e-6;
   integralGain                2e-4;
   maxRamp                     1e-4;
   minActuatingVariable        0;
   maxActuatingVariable        1e-3;
}

PhysicsParameters
{
   enableWetting     false;
   timesteps         10000;
   Re                3100;
   Fr                1.31;
   We                15.6188;
   useHardcodedUnits true;              // keep physical constants valid while U = 0
}

ParticleParameters
{
   inFileName spheres_out.dat;
   bedCopiesInX 1;
   bedCopiesInY 1;
   densityRatio 2.55;
   fixingHeightFactor 1.5;
   frictionCoefficient 0.5;
   restitutionCoefficient 0.97;
   numSubCycles 10;
   useLubricationCorrection true;
   useNoSlipParticles false;
   useParticleBed false;
}

